+---
+title: "best-open-source-ai-agents-2026"
+description: "Discover the best open source AI agents in 2026, including self-hosted agent frameworks, coding agents, browser agents, and workflow tools on GitHub."
+icon: 📋
+category: AI Agents
+---
+
+# Best Open Source AI Agents 2026
+
+[![Awesome](https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/sindresorhus/awesome@main/media/badge.svg)](https://github.com/sindresorhus/awesome)
+[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT)
+![Topics](https://img.shields.io/github/stars/GagnDeep/awesome-best-open-source-ai-agents-2026?style=social)
+
+> This curated list of the best open source AI agents 2026 highlights serious GitHub projects for building, running, and self-hosting agent systems. It focuses on public repositories only, excludes proprietary SaaS-only products, and surfaces practical tools for agent frameworks, coding agents, browser agents, local assistants, and workflow automation.
+
+## Table of Contents
+- [TL;DR](#tldr)
+- [Why This List](#why-this-list)
+- [Open Source Tools](#open-source-tools)
+- [FAQ](#faq)
+- [Contributing](#contributing)
+- [License](#license)
+
+## TL;DR
+- **Best framework stack:** `google/adk-python`, `google/adk-go`, and `VoltAgent/voltagent` are strong starting points for code-first agent orchestration.
+- **Best coding agents:** `block/goose`, `langchain-ai/open-swe`, `plandex-ai/plandex`, and `QwenLM/qwen-code` are the most useful repos here for software tasks.
+- **Best browser and computer-use tools:** `bytedance/UI-TARS-desktop`, `trycua/cua`, `magnitudedev/browser-agent`, and `steel-dev/steel-browser` cover desktop and web automation well.
+- **Best self-hosted assistants:** `mudler/LocalAI`, `danny-avila/LibreChat`, `khoj-ai/khoj`, `eosphoros-ai/DB-GPT`, and `arc53/DocsGPT` are strong open-source alternatives to hosted AI products.
+- **Best workflow layer:** `triggerdotdev/trigger.dev`, `agent-sh/agentsys`, `rowboatlabs/rowboat`, `ghostwright/ghost-os`, and `cgoinglove/better-chatbot` bring orchestration, UI, and agent operations together.
+
+## Why This List
+The best open source AI agents give you control over code, deployment, data flow, model choice, and extensibility. That matters if you want self-hosted AI agents, auditable workflows, local inference, GitHub-native contribution paths, or infrastructure that can outlive any single vendor.
+
+This list is intentionally strict: every entry has a public `github.com` repository, every repo is open source, and each item includes stars, language, license, and a verified last commit date. Star counts and license metadata come from the research snapshot supplied for March 29, 2026, while last commit dates were checked against GitHub commit history pages during assembly.
+
+## Open Source Tools
+
+### Agent Frameworks
+
+#### [Google ADK Python](https://github.com/google/adk-python)
+> **Description:** The README presents ADK as an open-source, code-first Python framework for building, evaluating, and deploying sophisticated AI agents with flexibility and control. It emphasizes modular multi-agent systems, tool ecosystems, evaluation flows, and deploy-anywhere patterns rather than a no-code wrapper. The project is model-agnostic even though it is optimized for Gemini, and it includes a built-in development UI plus examples for single-agent and multi-agent compositions. Last commit date: Mar 18, 2026. Stars count: 18,653. Primary language: Python. License type: Apache-2.0.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/google/adk-python](https://github.com/google/adk-python)
+- **Stars:** 18,653 ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** Apache-2.0
+- **Last Commit:** Mar 18, 2026
+- **Category:** framework, multi-agent
+- **Best for:** Teams that want a code-first agent SDK with evaluation and deployment support
+
+---
+
+#### [Google ADK Go](https://github.com/google/adk-go)
+> **Description:** ADK Go extends the Agent Development Kit approach into Go for developers who want typed services, agent tooling, and orchestration in a compiled runtime. Its positioning is similar to the Python repo, but the main appeal is integrating agent systems into Go-heavy backends and infrastructure workflows. The commit history shows active work on remote agents, debug telemetry, debug endpoints, and tool confirmation behavior, which makes it useful for production-oriented services rather than demos alone. Last commit date: Mar 3, 2026. Stars count: 7,276. Primary language: Go. License type: Apache-2.0.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/google/adk-go](https://github.com/google/adk-go)
+- **Stars:** 7,276 ⭐
+- **Language:** Go
+- **License:** Apache-2.0
+- **Last Commit:** Mar 3, 2026
+- **Category:** framework, sdk
+- **Best for:** Go teams building agent backends and remote-agent services
+
+---
+
+#### [VoltAgent](https://github.com/VoltAgent/voltagent)
+> **Description:** VoltAgent is a TypeScript agent framework aimed at developers who want a modern runtime for LLM apps, agent flows, tool execution, and observability. Its recent commit history shows active work around prompt context telemetry, versioning, and core prompt handling, which fits its positioning as an agent engineering framework rather than a simple starter template. The repo is approachable for Node.js teams and is especially relevant if you want strong TypeScript ergonomics with agent building blocks and production-minded release cadence. Last commit date: Mar 21, 2026. Stars count: 7,117. Primary language: TypeScript. License type: MIT.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/VoltAgent/voltagent](https://github.com/VoltAgent/voltagent)
+- **Stars:** 7,117 ⭐
+- **Language:** TypeScript
+- **License:** MIT
+- **Last Commit:** Mar 21, 2026
+- **Category:** framework, typescript
+- **Best for:** Node and TypeScript teams shipping agent products quickly
+
+---
+
+#### [SuperAGI](https://github.com/TransformerOptimus/SuperAGI)
+> **Description:** SuperAGI is one of the earlier open-source agent platforms focused on autonomous task execution, tool usage, and agent management from a unified system. The project remains relevant because it bundles orchestration concepts, UI, and operational ideas that many newer repos still borrow from. Its recent GitHub activity is slower than the fastest-moving projects on this list, so it is better treated as a mature reference implementation than the default first choice for a fresh stack. Last commit date: Jan 22, 2025. Stars count: 17,385. Primary language: Python. License type: MIT.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/TransformerOptimus/SuperAGI](https://github.com/TransformerOptimus/SuperAGI)
+- **Stars:** 17,385 ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** MIT
+- **Last Commit:** Jan 22, 2025
+- **Category:** framework, autonomous-agents
+- **Best for:** Exploring a broad autonomous-agent platform with a large community footprint
+
+---
+
+#### [OpenAgentsControl](https://github.com/darrenhinde/OpenAgentsControl)
+> **Description:** OpenAgentsControl describes itself as an AI agent framework for plan-first development workflows with approval-based execution. The README and recent commits emphasize multi-language support, validation, code review, and predictable execution rather than raw chat UX. That makes it a good fit for engineering-heavy teams that want agents to operate inside explicit workflow steps with governance and testing built in. It is newer and smaller than ADK or VoltAgent, but it is opinionated in a useful way. Last commit date: Feb 20, 2026. Stars count: 3,115. Primary language: TypeScript. License type: MIT.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/darrenhinde/OpenAgentsControl](https://github.com/darrenhinde/OpenAgentsControl)
+- **Stars:** 3,115 ⭐
+- **Language:** TypeScript
+- **License:** MIT
+- **Last Commit:** Feb 20, 2026
+- **Category:** framework, plan-first
+- **Best for:** Approval-based engineering workflows and controlled agent execution
+
+---
+
+### Coding Agents
+
+#### [Open SWE](https://github.com/langchain-ai/open-swe)
+> **Description:** Open SWE is an open-source software engineering agent focused on practical coding workflows such as repository operations, issue handling, PR review tasks, and external work integrations. The commit log shows ongoing additions for GitHub review tooling, Slack support, Linear integration, and model configuration, which aligns with its positioning as an applied dev agent rather than a generic chatbot. It is one of the clearest repos to study if you want an agent that behaves like a software teammate with structured tools. Last commit date: Mar 25, 2026. Stars count: 8,804. Primary language: Python. License type: MIT.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/langchain-ai/open-swe](https://github.com/langchain-ai/open-swe)
+- **Stars:** 8,804 ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** MIT
+- **Last Commit:** Mar 25, 2026
+- **Category:** coding-agent, software-engineering
+- **Best for:** GitHub-centric engineering automation and issue-to-PR workflows
+
+---
+
+#### [Goose](https://github.com/block/goose)
+> **Description:** Goose is a Rust-based open-source coding agent and assistant with a strong CLI and TUI focus. Its velocity is high, and the recent commit history shows constant refinement to the terminal experience, documentation, and interactive tooling. The repo is attractive if you want a local-first or developer-first agent surface that feels like a real tool rather than a hosted wrapper. Its large community and active maintenance make it one of the safest picks on this list for coding workflows in 2026. Last commit date: Mar 29, 2026. Stars count: 33,737. Primary language: Rust. License type: Apache-2.0.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/block/goose](https://github.com/block/goose)
+- **Stars:** 33,737 ⭐
+- **Language:** Rust
+- **License:** Apache-2.0
+- **Last Commit:** Mar 29, 2026
+- **Category:** coding-agent, cli
+- **Best for:** Developers who want a fast local coding assistant with terminal ergonomics
+
+---
